QB.add(18,[{"id":18004,"t":"What Is Molecular Cell Biology?","f":"mc06","s":"Why is a Parkinson's disease molecular pathway clinically relevant?","o":["Disease rests on molecular pathways, which are causes or drug targets","Disease rests on molecular pathways that must be memorised in full","Parkinson’s is purely genetic, with no other causes","Parkinson’s has no molecular basis yet identified","To introduce CRISPR gene editing as the established cure for Parkinson’s"],"e":"“Don't worry about memorising this pathway.” It highlights that <b>disease has its foundations at the molecular and cellular level</b>: membrane receptors, <b>ion (calcium) accumulation</b>, <b>excitotoxicity</b>, <b>inflammation</b>, <b>protein (α-synuclein) accumulation</b>, mitochondrial dysfunction and oxidative stress, leading to death of dopaminergic neurons. Understanding disease allows development of therapeutics.","w":["Don’t memorise.","Not.","It has.","Not."],"k":"Disease has molecular foundations.","rn":[["","Where does disease have its foundations?","Where does disease have its foundations?","Molecular level; Cellular level"],["","Parkinson's: which 2 membrane processes are causes/targets?","Parkinson's: which 2 membrane processes are causes/targets?","Membrane receptors; Calcium channels, accumulation"],["","Parkinson's: which 3 other processes are causes/targets?","Parkinson's: which 3 other processes are causes/targets?","Excitotoxic processes; Inflammation; Protein accumulation"]]},{"id":18005,"t":"What Is Molecular Cell Biology?","s":"Which disease categories are given as examples whose understanding at the molecular level allows development of therapeutics?","o":["Infectious, autoimmune, cancer and genetic disease","Infectious, autoimmune, cancer and traumatic disease","Cancer and genetic disease only","Infectious disease only","Psychiatric disease only"],"e":"Understanding disease allows for the development of therapeutics, e.g. <b>infectious, autoimmune, cancer and genetic</b> disease.","w":["Trauma not listed.","More.","More.","Not."],"k":"Infectious, autoimmune, cancer, genetic.","rn":[["","Which 4 disease types does molecular cell biology help explain?","Which 4 disease types does molecular cell biology help explain?","Infectious; Autoimmune; Cancer; Genetic disease"]]},{"id":18006,"t":"What Is Molecular Cell Biology?","s":"Why should clinicians understand molecular cell biology when hearing about new therapies?","o":["To be realistic, as many therapies over-promise","To develop new drugs themselves in the lab","To avoid the need for clinical trials","Because pharmacology is not otherwise taught in the programme","Because MCB replaces examination"],"e":"Understanding receptors, signalling, DNA, proteins and cellular processes helps you understand disease and <b>how therapies work</b>, and to be <b>realistic</b>: many are privately developed and sometimes <b>over-promise</b>. You then participate in this knowledge rather than observing from afar.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not the reason.","Not."],"k":"MCB helps judge therapies realistically.","rn":[["","Why should clinicians know how a therapy works?","Why should clinicians know how a therapy works?","More realistic expectations"]]},{"id":18007,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"Name: an objectively measured indicator of normal, pathogenic or drug-response processes.","e":"This is the Biomarkers Definitions Working Group definition of a biomarker.","k":"Biomarker.","a":["biomarker","biomarkers","biological marker"],"rn":[["","Biomarker","Biomarker","Objectively measured characteristic; An indicator"],["","What can a biomarker indicate?","What can a biomarker indicate?","Normal biological processes; Pathogenic processes; Drug responses"]]},{"id":18008,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"A research group proposes protein X as a biomarker after studying 200 patients. What is needed before it can be used?","o":["Not enough – thousands of people and independent verification over years","Not enough – but one more study of 200 patients would confirm it as a biomarker","Enough – 200 patients define a biomarker if results are significant","Enough – biomarkers need no independent verification","Not enough – animal data must come first"],"e":"Defining a biomarker takes <b>years, perhaps decades</b>, including <b>independent verification</b>, large numbers of patients/volunteers and large data sets to find a <b>pattern</b>; thousands or tens of thousands, “a few hundred is not enough”.","w":["Thousands.","Not.","Verification.","Not stated."],"k":"Biomarkers need huge datasets.","rn":[["","How many people are needed to define a biomarker?","How many people are needed to define a biomarker?","Thousands to tens of thousands; A few hundred: not enough"]]},{"id":18009,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"Why can biomarkers give an earlier prognosis than symptoms or signs?","o":["Levels shift from baseline before signs appear","Levels change only after symptoms are established","Levels replace the need for a history and examination in most cases","Levels are always genetic and fixed from birth","Levels are unrelated to the disease process"],"e":"A biomarker has a <b>baseline</b> (normal values in health) that <b>changes</b> with pathology or treatment; levels often <b>change before</b> anything is visible, giving earlier prognosis. They are mostly <b>proteins</b> we all have.","w":["Before.","Not.","Not.","Related."],"k":"Biomarkers change early."},{"id":18010,"t":"Biomarkers","f":"mc08","s":"A breast cancer is ER-positive and PR-positive but HER2-negative. How do these biomarkers relate to prognosis?","o":["ER+ and PR+ mean better survival; HER2− avoids the worse HER2+ outlook","ER+ and PR+ mean worse survival; HER2− avoids the worse HER2+ outlook","ER+ and PR+ mean better survival; HER2− is the most aggressive type","ER+ and PR+ have no prognostic value","PR+ means lower survival than PR−"],"e":"<b>PR+</b>: higher survival than PR−. <b>ER+</b>: better survival than ER−. <b>High BRCA1</b>: worse prognosis for untreated patients. <b>HER2+</b>: worse prognosis, more aggressive than HER2−. Memory aid: hormone receptors positive = good news; HER2+ or high BRCA1 = bad news.","w":["Better.","HER2+ is worse.","They do.","Higher."],"k":"ER/PR+ good; HER2+/high BRCA1 bad.","rn":[["","Which 2 breast cancer biomarkers mean worse prognosis?","Which 2 breast cancer biomarkers mean worse prognosis?","High BRCA1; HER2-positive"],["","Which 2 breast cancer biomarkers mean better survival when positive?","Which 2 breast cancer biomarkers mean better survival when positive?","PR; ER"]]},{"id":18011,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"What does high BRCA1 protein expression indicate in breast cancer?","o":["Worse prognosis for untreated patients","Better prognosis for untreated patients","Worse prognosis only after treatment","HER2 positivity","Triple-negative status"],"e":"<b>High BRCA1 expression</b> indicates <b>worse prognosis</b> for untreated patients. The BRCA1 protein is the product of translating the BRCA1 gene.","w":["Worse.","Untreated.","Different marker.","Not."],"k":"High BRCA1 = worse prognosis.","rn":[["","What does high BRCA1 expression indicate?","What does high BRCA1 expression indicate?","Worse prognosis for untreated patients"]]},{"id":18012,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"Besides prognosis, what else do breast cancer biomarkers help with?","o":["Choosing treatment and its intensity by subtype","Diagnosing any infection present alongside the cancer","Predicting future fractures","Replacing the need for surgery","Measuring tumour size on imaging"],"e":"Testing biomarkers helps <b>define the treatment</b> and <b>how aggressive</b> it should be; breast cancer has <b>molecular subtypes</b> and biomarkers show which subtype a patient has.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Biomarkers guide treatment choice."},{"id":18013,"t":"Biomarkers","f":"mc09","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> a clinical use of biomarkers listed?","o":["Performing surgery","Diagnosis","Prediction of drug response","Treatment response","Disease progression"],"e":"Biomarker uses: <b>diagnosis</b>, <b>prognosis</b>, <b>prediction of drug response</b>, <b>treatment response</b>, <b>disease progression</b>. Biomarkers are objective and quantifiable.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed."],"k":"Five biomarker uses."},{"id":18014,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"Why are blood-based biomarkers attractive compared with biopsy?","o":["A blood test is easy and less invasive than biopsy","A blood test shows the tissue architecture","A blood test is always cheaper than any other test","A blood test needs no patient involvement","A blood test is more invasive but more accurate"],"e":"Biomarkers often only need a <b>blood test</b>, which is easy, compared with more <b>invasive</b> approaches such as biopsies.","w":["Not.","Not stated.","Not.","Less invasive."],"k":"Blood biomarkers: less invasive.","rn":[["","Why is a blood biomarker better than a biopsy?","Why is a blood biomarker better than a biopsy?","Easy; Less invasive"]]},{"id":18015,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"Name the protein commonly measured in blood as a biomarker of inflammation.","e":"CRP (C-reactive protein) is an inflammatory biomarker; blood-based biomarkers are easy and less invasive than biopsy.","k":"CRP.","a":["crp","c-reactive protein","c reactive protein"],"rn":[["","CRP is an example of what?","CRP is an example of what?","A biomarker (protein level); Used in inflammatory conditions"]]},{"id":18016,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"Why is there a big effort to find biomarkers for neurodegenerative diseases such as Alzheimer's?","o":["Damage precedes symptoms; early markers allow earlier treatment","Damage only follows symptoms, so early markers are not really needed","They are easy to biopsy, so markers are cheap","Treatments already cure them once diagnosed","They are infections detectable by biomarkers"],"e":"For <b>neurodegeneration</b>, by the time symptoms appear the damage has happened and treatments <b>delay rather than cure</b>; reliable <b>early biomarkers</b> would allow treatment to slow progression.","w":["Damage occurs first.","Not.","Delay, not cure.","Not."],"k":"Neurodegeneration: need early biomarkers.","rn":[["","Why are biomarkers vital in neurodegeneration?","Why are biomarkers vital in neurodegeneration?","Symptoms = damage done; Treatments delay, not cure"],["","Why are biomarkers useful for early disease?","Why are biomarkers useful for early disease?","Earlier than symptoms; Catch early stages"]]},{"id":18017,"t":"Translational Research","s":"Name the research that applies basic lab findings in the clinic, in a loop back to the lab.","e":"Translational research: lab → trials → patients → practice → back to the lab.","k":"Translational research.","a":["translational research","translational medicine","translational"],"rn":[["","TR","TR","Translational research"],["","Is translational research one-way?","Is translational research one-way?","No; A cycle that feeds back"],["","Translational research","Translational research","Basic research applied; To clinical setting"]]},{"id":18018,"t":"Translational Research","s":"Which are the examples of translational research listed?","o":["SARS-CoV-2 research, ADC therapy, TNF and IL-1 inhibitors","SARS-CoV-2 research, CRISPR gene editing, TNF and IL-1 inhibitors","Gene therapy, ADC therapy and biomarkers only","Vaccines only","Surgery only"],"e":"Examples of TR: labs worldwide researching <b>SARS-CoV-2</b>; <b>selective targeting of cancer cells (antibody–drug conjugate therapy)</b>; <b>TNF and IL-1 inhibitors</b>.","w":["CRISPR not listed.","Not.","More.","Not."],"k":"TR examples.","rn":[["","Give 3 examples of TR (translational research).","Give 3 examples of TR (translational research).","SARS-CoV-2 research; Antibody-drug conjugates; TNF and IL-1 inhibitors"]]},{"id":18019,"t":"Translational Research","f":"mc11","s":"A new drug is first given to a small number of people to check its toxicity, then whether it does anything. Which phases are these?","o":["Phase 1 and 2","Phase 3","Phase 4","Preclinical only","Policy stage"],"e":"From the recording: basic research → <b>phase 1 and 2</b>: <b>small numbers</b>; first <b>toxicity</b>, then <b>efficacy</b>. <b>Phase 3</b>: more patients, reported to health services; may inform policy (approvals, <b>NICE</b>). <b>Phase 4</b>: observational studies, with constant feedback.","w":["More patients.","Observational after use.","Before humans.","After trials."],"k":"Phase 1–2: small, toxicity then efficacy.","rn":[["","What is phase 1 of clinical trials?","What is phase 1 of clinical trials?","Small number of patients; First check toxicity"]]},{"id":18020,"t":"Translational Research","s":"Which UK body typically appraises a new drug after phase 3 results to inform policy and practice?","e":"NICE comes in after phase 3; phase 4 is observational post-marketing reporting.","k":"NICE.","a":["nice","national institute for health and care excellence","national institute for health and clinical excellence"],"rn":[["","Who decides if a treatment is used for all patients?","Who decides if a treatment is used for all patients?","NICE"],["","How are standard drug doses set?","How are standard drug doses set?","Guidelines (NICE); From thousands of patients"]]},{"id":18021,"t":"Translational Research","s":"Which clinical-trial phase consists of observational studies after a drug is in routine use, reporting back?","e":"Phase 1: toxicity; 2: efficacy (small numbers); 3: larger numbers; 4: observational after use.","k":"Phase 4.","a":["phase 4","4","phase iv","iv","phase four"],"rn":[["","What is phase 1 of clinical trials?","What is phase 1 of clinical trials?","Small number of patients; First check toxicity"]]},{"id":18022,"t":"TNF and IL-1","f":"mc12","s":"Which statement about tumour necrosis factor is correct?","o":["An inflammatory cytokine mainly from macrophages, binding TNFR1 and TNFR2","An inflammatory cytokine mainly from neurons, binding TNFR1 and TNFR2","An inflammatory cytokine mainly from macrophages, binding IL-1RI","An anti-inflammatory hormone from the liver","A receptor on T cells"],"e":"<b>TNF</b>: cytokine involved in inflammation; primarily generated in <b>macrophages</b>; binds <b>TNFR1</b> and <b>TNFR2</b>; this pathway <b>initiates inflammation</b>. One of the primary molecules in early inflammation, studied early so its role was well documented.","w":["Macrophages.","TNFR1/2.","Inflammatory.","Cytokine."],"k":"TNF: macrophages, TNFR1/2, inflammation.","rn":[["","What are the 2 receptors for TNF?","What are the 2 receptors for TNF?","TNFR1; TNFR2"],["","TNF vs IL-1: receptors","TNF vs IL-1: receptors","TNF: TNFR1 and TNFR2; IL-1: IL-1RI"]]},{"id":18023,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"A patient with Crohn's disease is offered a biological medicine that neutralises an early inflammatory cytokine from macrophages. Name the drug class.","e":"Anti-TNF therapy is used in RA, ankylosing spondylitis, psoriasis, Crohn's and UC.","k":"Anti-TNF.","a":["anti-tnf","anti tnf","tnf inhibitor","tnf inhibitors","anti-tnf therapy","anti-tnf antibody","tnf blocker","anti-tnf-alpha"],"rn":[["","Crohn's disease can be treated with which biological?","Crohn's disease can be treated with which biological?","Anti-TNF"],["","Ankylosing spondylitis can be treated with which biological?","Ankylosing spondylitis can be treated with which biological?","Anti-TNF"],["","Anti-TNF: which 3 other diseases?","Anti-TNF: which 3 other diseases?","Psoriasis; Crohn's disease; Ulcerative colitis"]]},{"id":18024,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"Which of these conditions is <b>not</b> listed for anti-TNF therapy?","o":["Gout","Rheumatoid arthritis","Ankylosing spondylitis","Ulcerative colitis","Psoriatic arthritis"],"e":"<b>Gout</b> is listed among IL-1-associated diseases, not anti-TNF uses.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed."],"k":"Gout = IL-1 list.","rn":[["","Gout is linked to which cytokine?","Gout is linked to which cytokine?","IL-1"],["","IL-1 diseases: which 2 common ones?","IL-1 diseases: which 2 common ones?","Gout; Type 2 diabetes"]]},{"id":18025,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"A child has recurrent unprovoked inflammatory episodes due to a TNFRSF1A mutation. Name the syndrome (abbreviation accepted).","e":"TRAPS (TNFR1-associated periodic syndrome) is treated with anti-TNF, plus anti-IL-1 in severe cases.","k":"TRAPS.","a":["traps","tnf receptor associated periodic syndrome","tnfr1-associated periodic syndrome","tnf receptor-associated periodic syndrome","tnfr1 associated periodic syndrome"],"rn":[["","TRAPS","TRAPS","TNFR1-associated periodic syndrome"],["","Severe TRAPS cases are treated with what?","Severe TRAPS cases are treated with what?","Anti-IL-1 therapy"],["","How is TRAPS treated?","How is TRAPS treated?","Anti-TNF therapy"]]},{"id":18026,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"Explain the translational logic that led to anti-TNF therapy.","o":["TNF is early in inflammation; blocking it blocks inflammation; antibodies were developed","TNF is late in inflammation; blocking it blocks infection; antibodies were developed","TNF is early in inflammation; its gene was edited out with CRISPR in patients with arthritis","TNF was first identified as a biomarker only","TNF was found to cure cancer"],"e":"The story: TNF is an early inflammatory molecule → “if we block it, do we block inflammation?” → yes → in <b>autoimmunity</b> (excessive inflammation) blocking it stops the excess. This led to <b>antibodies against TNF</b>, now widely used <b>biological medicines</b>.","w":["Early.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Anti-TNF translational story.","rn":[["","Why does blocking TNF help autoimmunity?","Why does blocking TNF help autoimmunity?","Excessive inflammation; Blocking an early molecule"],["","What was the key question in the TNF story?","What was the key question in the TNF story?","Block TNF = block inflammation?"]]},{"id":18027,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"What evidence exists about anti-TNF beyond autoimmune disease?","o":["It may help in SARS-CoV-2 infection (Robinson et al. 2020)","It may help in Parkinson’s disease (Robinson et al. 2020)","It may prevent breast cancer","It may replace CRISPR therapy","It may treat deafness"],"e":"Notes: evidence that anti-TNF therapy may be useful in <b>SARS-CoV-2</b> infection (Robinson et al. 2020, Lancet Rheumatology).","w":["SARS-CoV-2.","Not.","Not.","Gene therapy."],"k":"Anti-TNF and SARS-CoV-2.","rn":[["","Anti-TNF may also be useful in which infection?","Anti-TNF may also be useful in which infection?","SARS-CoV-2 (COVID-19)"]]},{"id":18028,"t":"TNF and IL-1","f":"mc13","s":"Which statement about IL-1 is correct?","o":["A cytokine from macrophages and other cells, binding IL-1RI","A cytokine from macrophages only, binding the TNFR1 and TNFR2 receptors","A cytokine from neurons, binding IL-1RI","An anti-inflammatory cytokine binding IL-1RI","A DNA-binding protein in macrophages"],"e":"<b>IL-1</b>: cytokine involved in inflammation; primarily from <b>macrophages</b> but also <b>endothelial and epithelial cells</b>; binds <b>IL-1RI</b>; initiates inflammation.","w":["That is TNF.","Neurons.","Inflammatory.","Not."],"k":"IL-1: IL-1RI.","rn":[["","TNF vs IL-1: main source","TNF vs IL-1: main source","TNF: macrophages; IL-1: macrophages, endothelial; And epithelial cells"],["","Besides macrophages, which 2 cells make IL-1?","Besides macrophages, which 2 cells make IL-1?","Endothelial cells; Epithelial cells"]]},{"id":18029,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"Which of these is listed as an IL-1-associated disease?","o":["Hidradenitis suppurativa","Crohn’s disease","Ankylosing spondylitis","Ulcerative colitis","Psoriatic arthritis"],"e":"IL-1 common diseases: <b>Still's disease, Schnitzler syndrome, hidradenitis suppurativa, gout, type 2 diabetes</b>. The others are anti-TNF indications.","w":["Anti-TNF.","Anti-TNF.","Anti-TNF.","Anti-TNF."],"k":"IL-1 diseases list.","rn":[["","IL-1 diseases: which 3 rarer ones?","IL-1 diseases: which 3 rarer ones?","Still's disease; Schnitzler syndrome; Hidradenitis suppurativa"],["","Hidradenitis suppurativa is linked to which cytokine?","Hidradenitis suppurativa is linked to which cytokine?","IL-1"]]},{"id":18030,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"What are the two IL-1 blockade options?","o":["Anti-IL1R1 antibody and recombinant IL-1Ra","Anti-TNF antibody and recombinant TNF receptor","Anti-IL1R1 antibody and CRISPR knockout of the IL-1 gene","ADC therapy and tamoxifen","Corticosteroids and NSAIDs"],"e":"IL-1 blockade: (1) <b>anti-IL1R1</b> – an antibody that blocks the IL-1 receptor; (2) <b>recombinant IL-1 receptor antagonist</b>.","w":["TNF.","Not listed.","Not.","Not."],"k":"IL-1 blockade options.","rn":[["","What are the 2 IL-1 blockade treatment options?","What are the 2 IL-1 blockade treatment options?","Anti-IL1R1; Recombinant IL-1 receptor antagonist"]]},{"id":18031,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"How does a recombinant IL-1 receptor given to a patient reduce IL-1 signalling?","o":["It mops up circulating IL-1 as a decoy","It blocks the cell’s own IL-1 receptor directly on the membrane","It edits the IL-1 gene so less is made","It kills the macrophages that make IL-1","It increases IL-1 breakdown in the liver"],"e":"From the recording: a <b>recombinant</b> IL-1 receptor (made in the lab) given to the patient <b>binds the circulating IL-1</b>, so it cannot bind the cellular receptors – a decoy that “mops up” the protein.","w":["That is anti-IL1R1.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Recombinant decoy mops up IL-1."},{"id":18032,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"Which mechanism describes anti-TNF, compared with anti-IL-1R1?","o":["Anti-TNF neutralises the protein; anti-IL-1R1 blocks the receptor","Anti-TNF blocks the receptor; anti-IL-1R1 neutralises the protein","Both block receptors on the cell surface","Both mop up proteins with recombinant decoys","Anti-TNF edits genes; anti-IL-1R1 blocks receptors"],"e":"Three ways: block the <b>receptor</b> (anti-IL-1R1); “<b>mop up</b>” with a recombinant decoy (IL-1 receptor antagonist); <b>neutralise the protein</b> with an antibody (anti-TNF). “Anti-” means an <b>antibody</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Different.","Different.","Not."],"k":"Three ways to block a pathway.","rn":[["","What are the 2 main ways to block a protein?","What are the 2 main ways to block a protein?","Mop it up; With recombinant molecule; Block its receptor"],["","How does anti-IL1R1 work?","How does anti-IL1R1 work?","Antibody blocks the IL-1 receptor; IL-1 cannot bind"]]},{"id":18033,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"Why is stopping a protein–receptor interaction described as “the easiest way” to stop a pathological process?","o":["Most processes start with receptor binding; block it","Most processes start inside the nucleus; blocking that stops the cascade","Receptors lie inside the nucleus, so are easy to reach","Proteins rarely bind receptors, so few targets exist","It kills all cells involved in the process"],"e":"Most biological processes start with a <b>protein binding a receptor</b> on the membrane, then something happens inside the cell; if pathological, the easiest way to stop it is to <b>stop that interaction</b>.","w":["Membrane binding first.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Block binding → stop process.","rn":[["","What is the easiest way to stop a pathological signal?","What is the easiest way to stop a pathological signal?","Stop protein binding receptor"],["","What is the first step of most biological processes in cells?","What is the first step of most biological processes in cells?","A protein binding a receptor"],["","How does anti-IL1R1 work?","How does anti-IL1R1 work?","Antibody blocks the IL-1 receptor; IL-1 cannot bind"]]},{"id":18034,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"What term describes a therapeutic protein identical to the body's own but made in the lab at scale?","e":"Recombinant proteins (e.g. IL-1Ra) are made in the lab at scale.","k":"Recombinant.","a":["recombinant","recombinant protein"],"rn":[["","Recombinant protein","Recombinant protein","Same as body's protein; Made artificially in lab"]]},{"id":18035,"t":"Antibody-Drug Conjugates","f":"mc15","s":"Put the steps of antibody–drug conjugate action in order.","o":["Binds antigen → internalised → payload released → cell killed","Binds antigen → payload released outside → internalised → cell killed","Internalised → binds antigen → payload released → cell killed","Payload enters nucleus → antibody binds DNA → cell killed","Binds antigen → cell killed by antibody alone"],"e":"<b>ADC</b> = antibody + linker + cytotoxic payload. It binds tumour surface antigen (B) → <b>receptor-mediated internalisation</b> (C) → decomposed to release the payload (via protease, acidic, reductive agents or lysosomal processing) → <b>cell death</b> (D). Efficacy depends on the tumour antigen, antibody, payload and conjugation strategy.","w":["Released inside.","Bind first.","Not.","Payload kills."],"k":"ADC: bind → internalise → release → kill.","rn":[["","ADC action: last 2 steps?","ADC action: last 2 steps?","Payload released; Tumour cell killed"],["","ADC action: first 2 steps?","ADC action: first 2 steps?","Binds target antigen; Receptor-mediated internalisation"]]},{"id":18036,"t":"Antibody-Drug Conjugates","s":"What does MCB contribute to designing an antibody–drug conjugate?","o":["Knowing which receptors cells carry, and how many","Knowing the patient’s diet and lifestyle","Knowing the full DNA sequence of the cytotoxic payload","Designing surgical instruments","Measuring blood pressure"],"e":"First you must know, through research, <b>which receptors</b> are on the cells and <b>how many</b> – understanding the cell in order to target it. ADCs aim to improve drug delivery targeting and overall therapeutic efficacy.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"ADC design needs receptor knowledge.","rn":[["","What must be known before an ADC is made?","What must be known before an ADC is made?","Which receptors on cells; How many"]]},{"id":18037,"t":"Antibody-Drug Conjugates","s":"What is the main aim of ADC therapy?","o":["Better targeted delivery and efficacy","To improve drug delivery equally to all cells in the body","To edit tumour DNA directly","To boost TNF in the tumour","To replace defective genes"],"e":"ADC: a unique approach to <b>improve drug delivery targeting</b> and therefore overall therapeutic <b>efficacy</b> – selective targeting of cancer cells.","w":["Selective.","CRISPR.","Not.","Gene therapy."],"k":"ADC = targeted delivery.","rn":[["","What is the aim of antibody-drug conjugates?","What is the aim of antibody-drug conjugates?","Improve drug delivery targeting; So improve therapeutic efficacy"]]},{"id":18038,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"Name the approach that finds subgroups of patients with distinct disease or treatment response.","e":"Stratified medicine defines subgroups; personalised medicine places an individual in one and tailors treatment.","k":"Stratified medicine.","a":["stratified medicine","stratification","stratified"],"rn":[["","Which comes first: stratified or personalised medicine?","Which comes first: stratified or personalised medicine?","Stratified (define subgroups first)"],["","Stratified medicine","Stratified medicine","Identifies population subgroups; Distinct disease or response"]]},{"id":18039,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"What proportions of breast cancers are PR+/ER+, HER2+ and triple negative?","o":["65–75%, 15–20% and 15%","15–20%, 65–75% and 15%","65–75%, 15% and 15–20%","50%, 25% and 25%","90%, 5% and 5%"],"e":"Breast cancer subgroups: <b>PR+/ER+ 65–75%</b>, <b>HER2+ 15–20%</b>, <b>triple negative 15%</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Swapped.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Breast cancer subgroup proportions.","rn":[["","Breast cancer subtype frequencies","Breast cancer subtype frequencies","PR+/ER+: 65-75%; HER2+: 15-20%; Triple negative: 15%"],["","What are the 3 breast cancer subgroups?","What are the 3 breast cancer subgroups?","PR+ ER+; HER2+; Triple negative"]]},{"id":18040,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"An ER-positive breast cancer patient and a HER2-positive patient are discussed. Which targeted treatment fits each?","o":["ER+ – tamoxifen; HER2+ – Herceptin","ER+ – Herceptin; HER2+ – tamoxifen","ER+ – tamoxifen; HER2+ – anti-TNF","Both – Herceptin","Both – tamoxifen"],"e":"Notes: <b>ER+</b> patients are treated with <b>tamoxifen</b>; <b>HER2+</b> patients can be given <b>Herceptin</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Herceptin.","ER+ tamoxifen.","HER2+ Herceptin."],"k":"ER+ tamoxifen; HER2+ Herceptin.","rn":[["","Herceptin is used for which breast cancer subgroup?","Herceptin is used for which breast cancer subgroup?","HER2-positive"]]},{"id":18041,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"Why did giving all breast cancer patients the same treatment decades ago work for some but not others?","o":["Similar cancers have different molecular causes, so respond differently","Similar cancers have identical causes, but patients differ in compliance","The treatments used were never effective","Breast cancer is not a molecular disease","Patients did not take the treatments"],"e":"Not all breast cancers have the same <b>molecular cause</b>; they may present similarly (lumps, metastases to lung or brain) but the <b>starting cause differs</b>, influencing metastasis and response. Looking at molecular identity showed which treatment works better in which group (e.g. triple negative).","w":["Different causes.","Effective for some.","It is.","Not."],"k":"Different molecular causes → different responses.","rn":[["","What is similar between breast cancers?","What is similar between breast cancers?","Manifestation (lumps); Metastasis (lungs, brain)"],["","Can patients in one subgroup respond differently to the same treatment?","Can patients in one subgroup respond differently to the same treatment?","Yes, some better, some worse"],["","Do all breast cancers have the same molecular cause?","Do all breast cancers have the same molecular cause?","No"]]},{"id":18042,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"How are patient subgroups mostly identified?","o":["Blood tests: proteins and white-cell DNA","Imaging: X-rays and CT scans of the primary tumour","Physical examination alone","Questionnaires about symptoms","Skin biopsies only"],"e":"Mostly <b>blood tests</b>: circulating proteins (biomarkers) and <b>DNA from white blood cells</b> for genetic testing, sequencing genes.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Subgroups via blood biomarkers and WBC DNA.","rn":[["","Where does DNA in a blood test come from?","Where does DNA in a blood test come from?","White blood cells"]]},{"id":18043,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"Colorectal cancer patients undergo molecular subtyping into subtypes 1–3 with subtype-specific biomarkers. What is the aim?","o":["To split patients by good or poor prognosis","To give all patients identical treatment","To edit the tumour genes found in each molecular subtype","To measure tumour size","To avoid treatment altogether"],"e":"CRC patients → molecular subtyping → subtypes 1–3 → <b>subtype-specific biomarkers</b> → <b>good vs poor prognosis</b> groups (Bramsen et al. 2017).","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"CRC subtyping → prognosis groups.","rn":[["","What do subtype-specific biomarkers do in CRC?","What do subtype-specific biomarkers do in CRC?","Split subtypes into; Good vs poor prognosis"]]},{"id":18044,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","f":"mc17","s":"Which statement best distinguishes personalised from stratified medicine?","o":["Stratified defines subgroups; personalised places a patient in one and tailors","Personalised defines subgroups; stratified places a patient in one and tailors","They are identical terms","Stratified uses one patient; personalised uses large groups","Personalised ignores molecular data"],"e":"<b>Stratified</b>: from a large group, define <b>subgroups</b> by molecular subtype. <b>Personalised</b>: a single patient's DNA/tumour is analysed, assigned to a subgroup and given a <b>tailor-made</b> treatment. Broadly interchangeable but personalised goes to <b>greater depth</b>; the lecturer stressed they are <b>not the same</b>, and stratified comes <b>first</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Different.","Swapped.","Not."],"k":"Stratify first, then personalise.","rn":[["","Stratified vs personalised: main job","Stratified vs personalised: main job","Stratified: define subgroups; Personalised: assign and tailor"],["","Which comes first: stratified or personalised medicine?","Which comes first: stratified or personalised medicine?","Stratified (define subgroups first)"],["","How is a single patient handled in personalised medicine?","How is a single patient handled in personalised medicine?","DNA sequenced; Assigned to a subgroup; Given tailor-made treatment"]]},{"id":18045,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"A patient's tumour is sequenced and modelled, and a drug chosen specifically for them. Name this approach.","e":"Personalised medicine tailors treatment to the individual (e.g. tumour genotyping).","k":"Personalised medicine.","a":["personalised medicine","personalized medicine","precision medicine","personalised","personalized"],"rn":[["","Personalised cancer medicine: first 3 steps?","Personalised cancer medicine: first 3 steps?","Cancer patients; Tumour; Sequencing"],["","Personalised cancer medicine: last 2 steps?","Personalised cancer medicine: last 2 steps?","Analysis, modelling; Tailored drug per patient"],["","Personalised medicine: cancer treatment","Personalised medicine: cancer treatment","Tumour genotyping; Precise characterisation and treatment options"]]},{"id":18046,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"Name the genomic application that uses a patient's genes to alter drug dosage and treatment regimes.","e":"Pharmacogenomics alters dosage and regimes using genomic data. Other applications: tumour genotyping, pathogen genotyping, disease prediction.","k":"Pharmacogenomics.","a":["pharmacogenomics","pharmacogenetics"],"rn":[["","Personalised medicine: pharmacogenetics and pharmacogenomics","Personalised medicine: pharmacogenetics and pharmacogenomics","Altered dosage and treatment regimes; Current and novel drugs"],["","Altering drug dosage by genes is called what?","Altering drug dosage by genes is called what?","Pharmacogenetics / pharmacogenomics"]]},{"id":18047,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"Knowing a virus's exact variant helps design a targeted vaccine. Name this genomic application.","e":"Pathogen genotyping identifies the exact variant of a pathogen.","k":"Pathogen genotyping.","a":["pathogen genotyping","pathogen genomics","viral genotyping"],"rn":[["","Personalised medicine: infectious agents","Personalised medicine: infectious agents","Pathogen genotyping; Precise targeted therapies; Potential vaccines"]]},{"id":18048,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"What is the aim of personalised prescribing compared with guideline doses?","o":["A cheap test setting each patient’s own dose","A cheap test confirming everyone needs the same guideline dose","Increasing doses for all patients","Avoiding NICE guidance","Stopping prescribing altogether"],"e":"Guideline doses are based on thousands of patients, but each responds differently. Personalised medicine aims for an <b>inexpensive test</b> defining <b>how much to give</b> each patient, based on molecular signature and <b>metabolism</b>, <b>minimising side effects</b>.","w":["Individual.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Personalised dosing.","rn":[["","What dosing test does personalised medicine aim for?","What dosing test does personalised medicine aim for?","Inexpensive test; Sets dose per patient"]]},{"id":18049,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"Why has personalised medicine moved from “science fiction” to reality over about 30 years?","o":["Cheaper sequencing, more storage, AI","Cheaper sequencing, fewer patients and no need for trials","The discovery of DNA and the microscope","More data storage alone","Abolition of clinical trials"],"e":"About 30 years ago it was science fiction because of <b>cost</b>. Now: <b>lower sequencing cost</b>, <b>more data storage</b> and <b>artificial intelligence</b> pattern-finding.","w":["Not.","Not.","More.","Not."],"k":"Cheap sequencing, storage, AI.","rn":[["","What 3 changes made personalised medicine real?","What 3 changes made personalised medicine real?","Lower sequencing cost; Huge increase in data storage; Artificial intelligence"]]},{"id":18050,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","f":"mc19","s":"Which statement describes gene therapy using an adenovirus vector?","o":["Engineered adenovirus delivers DNA to the nucleus without integrating","Engineered adenovirus integrates DNA into every chromosome","Engineered adenovirus delivers protein directly into the nucleus","Cas9 and guide RNA are delivered to edit and permanently correct DNA in the nucleus","A cytotoxic payload kills the defective cells"],"e":"Gene therapy: <b>replace a defective gene with a functional one</b>. A carrier (<b>vector</b>) is engineered to deliver the gene; an <b>adenovirus</b> enters the cell, is packaged in a vesicle, acidity releases pentons and it breaches the vesicle, docks at the nuclear pore, and delivers DNA into the nucleus – <b>not integrated into a chromosome</b>; the gene is transcribed into mRNA.","w":["Not integrated.","DNA.","CRISPR.","ADC."],"k":"Adenovirus vector: DNA not integrated.","rn":[["","Is adenovirus-delivered DNA integrated into a chromosome?","Is adenovirus-delivered DNA integrated into a chromosome?","No"]]},{"id":18051,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","s":"Gene therapy can cost millions. Why can it still be cost-effective on the NHS?","o":["It can be value for money versus lifelong care","It is cheaper per dose than common painkillers used long term","It needs no follow-up after treatment","It is research only, not NHS care","It is not offered on the NHS"],"e":"Gene therapy is <b>very expensive</b> (millions), but compared with lifelong treatment it is <b>cost-effective</b> in some cases; now offered on the NHS. Very good results in <b>deafness</b>.","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","It is.","It is."],"k":"Gene therapy: costly but cost-effective."},{"id":18052,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","s":"Which enhancements and alternatives to gene therapy are listed?","o":["Retroviral elements as enhancements; CRISPR as an alternative","CRISPR as an enhancement; retroviral elements as an alternative","Antibodies and ADCs as enhancements","Biomarkers as alternatives","Surgery as an alternative"],"e":"Gene therapy: enhancements include <b>retroviral elements</b>; alternatives include protocols such as <b>CRISPR</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Retroviral elements; CRISPR.","rn":[["","What are gene therapy enhancements?","What are gene therapy enhancements?","Use of retroviral elements"]]},{"id":18053,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","s":"Name the DNA-binding protein that a guide RNA directs to a specific DNA sequence in genome editing.","e":"CRISPR/Cas9: Cas9 (DNA-binding protein) plus a guide RNA targeting a sequence next to a 5′-NGG-3′ PAM.","k":"Cas9.","a":["cas9","cas 9","cas-9"],"rn":[["","What are the 2 components of CRISPR/Cas9?","What are the 2 components of CRISPR/Cas9?","Cas9 (DNA binding protein); RNA molecule \"the guide\""],["","What is Cas9?","What is Cas9?","A DNA binding protein"]]},{"id":18054,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","s":"How can CRISPR/Cas9 be used for gene control rather than editing?","o":["By adding proteins that turn genes on and off","By adding proteins that permanently change DNA bases","By adding cytotoxic payloads","By adding an adenovirus vector","By measuring biomarkers"],"e":"Add proteins that <b>selectively change DNA base pairs</b> → precise <b>gene editing</b>; add proteins that <b>turn genes on and off</b> → precise <b>gene control</b>.","w":["Editing.","ADC.","Gene therapy.","Not."],"k":"CRISPR editing vs control.","rn":[["","Add proteins that turn genes on and off to Cas9: result?","Add proteins that turn genes on and off to Cas9: result?","Precise gene control system"]]},{"id":18055,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","s":"Name the PAM sequence targeted by the CRISPR/Cas9 guide.","e":"The PAM is 5′-NGG-3′.","k":"5′-NGG-3′.","a":["ngg","5-ngg-3","5'-ngg-3'","5′-ngg-3′","5 ngg 3"],"rn":[["","What is the PAM sequence?","What is the PAM sequence?","5'-NGG-3'"],["","What sequences does the guide target?","What sequences does the guide target?","PAM sequences; 5'-NGG-3'"]]},{"id":18056,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","s":"For which type of pathology is CRISPR best suited?","o":["Those due to one or a few specific mutations","Those with unknown or multifactorial causes","Traumatic injuries","All infections","Psychological disorders"],"e":"CRISPR works best when a pathology can be traced to <b>one gene or a few genes</b> with specific mutations; it does not work where the cause is unknown. If done early in enough cells: “a cure, a cure forever”.","w":["Does not work there.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"CRISPR: single-gene mutations.","rn":[["","Gene therapy works for diseases caused by what?","Gene therapy works for diseases caused by what?","A specific mutation"]]},{"id":18057,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","f":"mc22","s":"In the genome editing workflow for hereditary blood disorders, what happens to patient cells?","o":["To iPSCs, corrected, differentiated, tested, transplanted","Reprogrammed to iPSCs, treated with anti-TNF, then transplanted back","Destroyed and replaced by donor cells","Infected with adenovirus only and returned","Frozen permanently"],"e":"Workflow: patient somatic cells → iPSCs, or HSCs collected → CRISPR/Cas9 gene addition, disruption or correction → corrected cells → differentiation → tested in mice → transplanted back. “For your interest.”","w":["CRISPR, not anti-TNF.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"CRISPR ex vivo workflow.","rn":[["","What are the 2 routes for CRISPR stem cell therapy?","What are the 2 routes for CRISPR stem cell therapy?","Correct patient HSCs; Somatic cells to iPSCs; Then corrected"],["","Where do corrected cells go next?","Where do corrected cells go next?","Transplanted back into the patient; Or into mice for analysis"]]},{"id":18061,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"A patient with triple-negative breast cancer asks why tamoxifen and Herceptin are not offered. What is the best explanation?","o":["Triple-negative tumours lack the ER and HER2 targets those drugs need","Triple-negative tumours have the targets, but the drugs are too toxic","Tamoxifen and Herceptin are gene therapies","Triple-negative disease is not a cancer","Those drugs are no longer used"],"e":"Stratified medicine: <b>ER+</b> → tamoxifen; <b>HER2+</b> → Herceptin. Triple-negative (15%) lacks these targets and forms a distinct subgroup in which other treatments work better.","w":["Lacks targets.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Triple negative: no ER/HER2 target.","rn":[["","What are the 3 breast cancer subgroups?","What are the 3 breast cancer subgroups?","PR+ ER+; HER2+; Triple negative"],["","Breast cancer subtype frequencies","Breast cancer subtype frequencies","PR+/ER+: 65-75%; HER2+: 15-20%; Triple negative: 15%"]]},{"id":18062,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"Which best summarises how translational research produced IL-1 blockade?","o":["IL-1 identified as a cytokine acting via IL-1RI; blocking it was tested and adopted","IL-1 identified as a breast cancer biomarker; blocking it was tested and adopted","IL-1 knocked out with CRISPR in patients, which was then tested and adopted clinically","IL-1 found in viruses and blocked with vaccines","IL-1 blockade was adopted before any lab research"],"e":"Logic: identify a key molecule in a pathway (IL-1 via IL-1RI) → block it (anti-IL-1R1 or recombinant IL-1 receptor antagonist) → trials → biological therapy for Still's disease, Schnitzler syndrome, hidradenitis suppurativa, gout, type 2 diabetes.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"IL-1 blockade translational logic."},{"id":18063,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"Which pair of molecules both bind cell surface receptors to initiate inflammation and are primarily from macrophages?","o":["TNF and IL-1","HER2 and BRCA1","Cas9 and guide RNA","ER and PR","CRP and Cas9"],"e":"Both <b>TNF</b> (TNFR1/2) and <b>IL-1</b> (IL-1RI) are inflammatory cytokines primarily from macrophages that initiate inflammation.","w":["Cancer biomarkers.","CRISPR.","Hormone receptors.","Not."],"k":"TNF and IL-1 = inflammatory cytokines.","rn":[["","TNF vs IL-1: receptors","TNF vs IL-1: receptors","TNF: TNFR1 and TNFR2; IL-1: IL-1RI"],["","Where is TNF primarily generated?","Where is TNF primarily generated?","Macrophages"],["","TNF vs IL-1: main source","TNF vs IL-1: main source","TNF: macrophages; IL-1: macrophages, endothelial; And epithelial cells"]]},{"id":18064,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"Which of the following is a biomarker in the sense used?","o":["HER2 protein level in a tumour","A patient’s reported pain score","An adenovirus vector","A guide RNA","A surgical margin"],"e":"A biomarker is objectively measured (e.g. <b>HER2</b>); symptoms are subjective; vectors and guide RNA are therapeutic tools.","w":["Subjective.","Tool.","Tool.","Not."],"k":"HER2 = biomarker.","rn":[["","CRP is an example of what?","CRP is an example of what?","A biomarker (protein level); Used in inflammatory conditions"],["","What are the 4 critical breast cancer protein biomarkers?","What are the 4 critical breast cancer protein biomarkers?","PR; ER; BRCA1; HER2"]]},{"id":18065,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"A drug works well in 70% of patients but causes side effects in others with the same diagnosis. Which approach would best address this?","o":["Stratified then personalised medicine","A higher dose for every patient","Stopping the drug for all patients","Gene therapy for every patient","Ignoring the variability"],"e":"Minute individual differences influence response. Stratified medicine defines subgroups; personalised medicine/pharmacogenomics tailors dose and regime to minimise side effects.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Variability → stratify/personalise.","rn":[["","Which comes first: stratified or personalised medicine?","Which comes first: stratified or personalised medicine?","Stratified (define subgroups first)"]]},{"id":18066,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"Why can individuals respond differently to the same treatment, even though the general sequence of proteins like IL-1 is the same in everyone?","o":["Tiny genetic differences make each person unique","Proteins like IL-1 differ completely between people","Treatment response is random","Only age determines response","Only body weight determines response"],"e":"The general sequence of a protein is the same in everyone, but <b>minute changes</b> make each person unique and can influence <b>how someone responds</b>.","w":["Same general sequence.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Minor genetic variation → different responses.","rn":[["","Are everyone's genes the same?","Are everyone's genes the same?","General sequence: same; Minute changes make unique"]]},{"id":18067,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"Which statement about biomarkers and commercial predictive tests was made?","o":["Some pathology must be developing to see biomarker changes","Biomarkers predict all future disease in healthy people, even with no pathology","Commercial tests are the same as NHS biomarkers","Commercial tests are always accurate","Biomarkers never change"],"e":"She distinguished NHS biomarker efforts from commercial tests claiming to predict disease: you need some <b>pathology developing</b> to see biomarker changes.","w":["Not.","Different.","Not.","They change."],"k":"Biomarkers need developing pathology.","rn":[["","Can a biomarker change without pathology developing?","Can a biomarker change without pathology developing?","No, pathology must be developing"]]},{"id":18068,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","s":"In gene therapy, what is the engineered carrier (e.g. an adenovirus) that delivers a gene into a cell called?","e":"A vector delivers the gene; an adenovirus vector enters the nucleus without integrating.","k":"Vector.","a":["vector","viral vector"],"rn":[["","Vector","Vector","Engineered carrier; Delivers new gene into cell"],["","What virus is used as the gene therapy vector?","What virus is used as the gene therapy vector?","Adenovirus"]]},{"id":18069,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","s":"Which statement contrasts gene therapy and CRISPR/Cas9 correctly?","o":["Gene therapy adds a gene; CRISPR targets the mutated DNA","Gene therapy targets the mutated DNA; CRISPR adds a gene","Both deliver cytotoxic payloads","Both always use adenovirus vectors","Neither affects DNA"],"e":"Gene therapy: <b>replace a defective gene with a functional one</b> (vector delivery). CRISPR: guide RNA directs Cas9 to the <b>mutated DNA</b>, cutting out the mutation to correct it, or controlling genes.","w":["Swapped.","ADC.","Not.","They do."],"k":"Gene therapy adds; CRISPR edits.","rn":[["","What is gene addition in CRISPR/Cas9 editing?","What is gene addition in CRISPR/Cas9 editing?","Adding new DNA"],["","What 3 things can CRISPR/Cas9 do to DNA?","What 3 things can CRISPR/Cas9 do to DNA?","Gene addition; Gene disruption; Gene correction"],["","Gene therapy","Gene therapy","Replace defective gene; With a functional one"]]},{"id":18070,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","s":"Which disease has shown very good results from gene therapy?","o":["Deafness","Gout","Psoriasis","Breast cancer","Parkinson's disease"],"e":"Gene therapy: very good results in <b>deafness</b>, correcting causative genes.","w":["IL-1.","Anti-TNF.","Stratified.","Pathway example."],"k":"Gene therapy: deafness.","rn":[["","Which condition has been treated successfully with gene therapy?","Which condition has been treated successfully with gene therapy?","Deafness"]]},{"id":18071,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"Which statement about the history of biologics like anti-TNF is correct?","o":["Fairly recent, not available in the 1950s","Available and widely used in clinics since the 1950s","Used since the 1800s","Made by plants","No longer used"],"e":"Recombinant biologics are routine now but were special when developed; historically fairly recent – not around in the 1950s.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Used."],"k":"Biologics are recent.","rn":[["","How old are these biological therapies?","How old are these biological therapies?","Fairly recent (decades); Not around in the 1950s"]]},{"id":18072,"t":"Antibody-Drug Conjugates","s":"Which statement about ADC payload release is correct?","o":["Inside the cell via protease, acid, reduction or lysosomes","Via cleavage of the linker in the blood, before the antibody binds","Via CRISPR cutting the linker","Never; the whole ADC kills the cell","Outside the cell, before binding"],"e":"Inside the cell the ADC is decomposed to release the payload via linker sensitivity to <b>protease, acidic or reductive</b> agents, or <b>lysosomal processing</b>.","w":["Inside.","Not.","Payload released.","Inside."],"k":"ADC payload release.","rn":[["","What releases the ADC payload?","What releases the ADC payload?","Linker sensitivity; Protease, acid, reducing agents; Lysosomal processing"]]},{"id":18073,"t":"Antibody-Drug Conjugates","s":"What determines clinical efficacy of an ADC?","o":["Tuning antigen, antibody, payload, conjugation","Tuning the dose alone","Tuning the patient’s age","Tuning the CRISPR guide RNA and the Cas9 delivery","Tuning the drug’s colour"],"e":"ADC efficacy depends on fine-tuning the <b>tumour antigen, targeting antibody, cytotoxic payload and conjugation strategy</b>.","w":["More.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"ADC efficacy factors.","rn":[["","What 4 things determine ADC clinical efficacy?","What 4 things determine ADC clinical efficacy?","Tumour antigen; Targeting antibody; Cytotoxic payload; Conjugation strategy"]]},{"id":18074,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"Which pair best matches term and definition?","o":["Pharmacogenomics – altering dose and regime using genomic data","Stratified medicine – tailoring therapy to a single patient","Recombinant – extracted naturally from donors","Biomarker – a drug used to treat disease","Translational research – research with no clinical aim"],"e":"<b>Pharmacogenomics</b>: altering dosage/regimes using genomic data. Biomarkers are measured indicators; recombinant = artificially synthesised; TR applies research clinically; stratified defines subgroups.","w":["Personalised.","Lab-made.","Measured indicator.","Clinical."],"k":"Term matching.","rn":[["","Personalised medicine: pharmacogenetics and pharmacogenomics","Personalised medicine: pharmacogenetics and pharmacogenomics","Altered dosage and treatment regimes; Current and novel drugs"],["","Altering drug dosage by genes is called what?","Altering drug dosage by genes is called what?","Pharmacogenetics / pharmacogenomics"]]},{"id":18075,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"A drug company wants to block a pathological receptor–ligand interaction. Which of the three strategies avoids targeting the cell receptor directly?","o":["A recombinant decoy that mops up ligand","An antibody against the cell’s own receptor","Gene therapy to remove the receptor","CRISPR editing of the receptor gene","An ADC targeting the receptor"],"e":"A recombinant decoy (e.g. IL-1 receptor antagonist) binds circulating ligand; an anti-protein antibody (anti-TNF) also targets the ligand; anti-receptor antibodies target the receptor.","w":["Targets the receptor.","Not.","Not.","Targets receptor."],"k":"Decoy mops up ligand."},{"id":18076,"t":"What Is Molecular Cell Biology?","s":"Which processes are studied as causes or drug targets in Parkinson's disease?","o":["Membrane receptors, calcium, excitotoxicity, inflammation, protein build-up","Membrane receptors, calcium, bacterial infection, inflammation, protein build-up","Bacterial infection only","Diet and trauma only","Skin changes only"],"e":"Parkinson's: <b>cell membrane receptors</b>, <b>ion (calcium) accumulation</b>, <b>excitotoxic</b> processes, <b>inflammation</b>, <b>accumulation of proteins</b>.","w":["Excitotoxicity.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"PD molecular processes.","rn":[["","Parkinson's: which 2 membrane processes are causes/targets?","Parkinson's: which 2 membrane processes are causes/targets?","Membrane receptors; Calcium channels, accumulation"]]},{"id":18079,"t":"Translational Research","s":"Which clinical-trial principle explains why phase 3 involves more patients than phase 1?","o":["Once safety and efficacy are shown, more are needed for policy","Phase 3 is preclinical and needs more animals","Phase 1 uses the largest numbers, to detect rare toxic effects","Phase 3 checks toxicity first","Numbers do not matter between phases"],"e":"Phase 1/2: small numbers (toxicity, efficacy); phase 3: more patients, reported to health services to inform policy.","w":["Not.","Small numbers.","Phase 1.","They do."],"k":"Phases scale up."},{"id":18080,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"Which of the following best defines “anti-” in drug names like anti-TNF?","o":["An antibody against the named molecule","An antibiotic against the named molecule","A recombinant decoy receptor","A gene knockout of the named molecule","An antioxidant"],"e":"“<b>Anti-</b>” means an <b>antibody</b>: anti-TNF = antibody to TNF; anti-IL-1R1 = antibody blocking the IL-1 receptor.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Anti- = antibody.","rn":[["","What are anti-TNF drugs?","What are anti-TNF drugs?","Antibodies against TNF; Biological medicines"]]},{"id":18081,"t":"Translational Research","s":"Which statement about lab-to-clinic feedback in translational research is correct?","o":["Information flows back to the lab as a loop","Information flows only from lab to clinic","Clinicians never inform the lab","Labs stop work after phase 1","Feedback is not permitted"],"e":"TR is a cycle: information goes <b>back</b> to the lab – new questions, improvements.","w":["Loop.","Loop.","Not.","Not."],"k":"TR is a loop.","rn":[["","What happens to patient information in TR?","What happens to patient information in TR?","Reported back to research; Improves, raises questions"]]},{"id":18082,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"Which statement best describes why HER2-positive patients benefit from stratification?","o":["HER2+ is aggressive but targetable with Herceptin","HER2+ has the best prognosis, so these patients need less treatment","HER2+ is aggressive and treated with tamoxifen","HER2+ is a TNF-driven disease","HER2+ is triple negative"],"e":"HER2+: worse prognosis, more aggressive; HER2+ patients can be given <b>Herceptin</b>.","w":["Worse.","Herceptin.","Not.","Not."],"k":"HER2+: aggressive, Herceptin.","rn":[["","HER2-positive vs HER2-negative patients","HER2-positive vs HER2-negative patients","HER2-positive: worse prognosis; More aggressive cancer"],["","Herceptin is used for which breast cancer subgroup?","Herceptin is used for which breast cancer subgroup?","HER2-positive"]]},{"id":18083,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","s":"Which explanation for the cost-effectiveness argument about gene therapy is correct?","o":["One-off cost versus lifetime disease costs","It is cheaper per dose than paracetamol","It needs no specialist input","It is free to the NHS","It is unregulated"],"e":"Gene therapy costs millions, but compared with lifelong treatment it is cost-effective in some cases.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cost vs lifetime."},{"id":18084,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","s":"What is the main advantage of CRISPR “if done early and in enough affected cells”?","o":["It could be a permanent cure","It could delay disease for a few years","It requires daily injections","It works for diseases of unknown cause","It acts as a biomarker"],"e":"If done early and in enough affected cells, “<b>a cure, a cure forever</b>”.","w":["Cure.","Not.","Specific mutation.","Not."],"k":"CRISPR: potential cure.","rn":[["","Why can CRISPR be a cure?","Why can CRISPR be a cure?","Corrects mutation for good; If early, many cells"]]},{"id":18087,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"A guideline recommends dose X for symptom X. What is the limitation of this?","o":["Doses come from thousands, but individuals differ","Doses are chosen at random by committees","Guidelines are wrong for most patients and should be ignored","Guidelines apply mainly to animals","NICE guidance does not exist"],"e":"Prescribing follows guidelines (e.g. NICE) based on thousands of patients, but everyone is an individual and responds differently.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Guidelines vs individuals.","rn":[["","How are standard drug doses set?","How are standard drug doses set?","Guidelines (NICE); From thousands of patients"],["","Why can standard doses fail?","Why can standard doses fail?","Everyone is an individual; Different responses"]]},{"id":18088,"t":"Antibody-Drug Conjugates","s":"Which component of an antibody–drug conjugate actually kills the tumour cell?","e":"The antibody binds the antigen, the ADC is internalised, and the cytotoxic payload is released inside the cell to kill it.","k":"Payload.","a":["payload","cytotoxic payload","the payload","drug","cytotoxic drug"],"rn":[["","ADC action: last 2 steps?","ADC action: last 2 steps?","Payload released; Tumour cell killed"],["","What does the antibody carry in an ADC?","What does the antibody carry in an ADC?","A small molecule drug; The cytotoxic payload"],["","What are the 4 parts of an ADC?","What are the 4 parts of an ADC?","Antibody (Ab); Cytotoxic payload; Linker; Targeted antigen"]]},{"id":18091,"t":"Translational Research","s":"A researcher shows that blocking a newly identified inflammatory protein reduces disease in mice. What is the next translational step?","o":["Phase 1 trials in small numbers to check toxicity","Phase 3 trials in large numbers of patients to inform NICE","Immediate NICE approval","A phase 4 observational study","Marketing to all patients"],"e":"Basic/preclinical research → T1: <b>phase 1 and 2</b> trials (toxicity then efficacy) → phase 3 → policy (NICE) → phase 4.","w":["Phase 1 first.","Not.","After use.","Not."],"k":"Preclinical → phase 1.","rn":[["","What is phase 1 of clinical trials?","What is phase 1 of clinical trials?","Small number of patients; First check toxicity"]]},{"id":18094,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"Which statement about ER as a biomarker is correct?","o":["ER+ patients survive better than ER−","ER+ patients survive worse than ER−","ER is a cytokine","ER is a CRISPR target","ER is a viral vector"],"e":"<b>ER+</b> patients have <b>better survival</b> than ER−.","w":["Better.","Receptor.","Not.","Not."],"k":"ER+ better survival.","rn":[["","ER-positive vs ER-negative patients","ER-positive vs ER-negative patients","ER-positive have better survival"],["","Which 2 breast cancer biomarkers mean better survival when positive?","Which 2 breast cancer biomarkers mean better survival when positive?","PR; ER"]]},{"id":18095,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","s":"Which statement about pathologies best suited to genetic therapies is correct?","o":["Those due to a specific mutation","Those with unknown causes","Purely psychological conditions","Fractures","Infections only"],"e":"Gene therapy/CRISPR are used for diseases whose pathology is down to a <b>specific mutation</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Genetic therapy: known mutation.","rn":[["","Gene therapy works for diseases caused by what?","Gene therapy works for diseases caused by what?","A specific mutation"]]},{"id":18096,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","s":"Which mechanism lets an adenovirus vector escape the vesicle after entering the cell?","o":["Rising acidity releases pentons and the virus breaches the vesicle","Rising alkalinity releases pentons and the virus breaches the vesicle","Cas9 cuts open the vesicle","TNF opens the vesicle","It is digested completely in the vesicle"],"e":"Inside the vesicle, increasing <b>acidity</b> releases <b>pentons</b> and the virus breaches the vesicle, then docks at the nuclear pore.","w":["Acidity.","Not.","Not.","It escapes."],"k":"Acidity → penton release.","rn":[["","Adenovirus gene therapy: escape steps?","Adenovirus gene therapy: escape steps?","Acidity releases pentons; Penton toxicity breaches vesicle"]]},{"id":18097,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"Which combination of biomarker results would most likely indicate a poorer-prognosis breast cancer?","o":["ER−, PR−, HER2+","ER+, PR+, HER2−","ER+, PR+, low BRCA1","PR+ only","ER+ only"],"e":"ER− and PR− lose the survival advantage; HER2+ indicates worse prognosis and more aggressive disease.","w":["Better.","Better.","Better.","Better."],"k":"HER2+, ER/PR− = poorer.","rn":[["","Name the breast cancer biomarkers","Name the breast cancer biomarkers","PR; ER; BRCA1; HER2"],["","What are the 4 critical breast cancer protein biomarkers?","What are the 4 critical breast cancer protein biomarkers?","PR; ER; BRCA1; HER2"],["","What are the 3 breast cancer subgroups?","What are the 3 breast cancer subgroups?","PR+ ER+; HER2+; Triple negative"]]},{"id":18098,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"Which use of biomarkers would assess whether a patient's chemotherapy is working?","o":["Treatment response","Diagnosis","Disease prediction in healthy people","Pathogen genotyping","Gene therapy"],"e":"<b>Treatment response</b> is a listed biomarker use.","w":["Different.","Not.","Infection.","Not."],"k":"Biomarker: treatment response.","rn":[["","Biomarkers: 2 uses about treatment?","Biomarkers: 2 uses about treatment?","Predict drug response; Treatment response"]]},{"id":18100,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"Which statement about tumour genotyping is correct?","o":["Characterising a cancer and its treatments","Precise characterisation of a virus for vaccine design","Replacing surgery in all cancers","Measuring CRP","A phase 4 trial"],"e":"<b>Cancer treatment</b>: <b>tumour genotyping</b> allows precise characterisation and subsequent treatment options for an individual.","w":["Pathogen.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Tumour genotyping.","rn":[["","Personalised medicine: cancer treatment","Personalised medicine: cancer treatment","Tumour genotyping; Precise characterisation and treatment options"]]},{"id":18101,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"Which statement about disease prediction as a genomic application is correct?","o":["Estimating disease likelihood for early action","Diagnosing an infection that is already established","Setting individual drug doses","Editing genes","Characterising a tumour"],"e":"<b>Disease prediction</b>: likelihood of developing a disease, allowing <b>early intervention</b>.","w":["Not.","Pharmacogenomics.","CRISPR.","Tumour genotyping."],"k":"Disease prediction.","rn":[["","Personalised medicine: disease prediction","Personalised medicine: disease prediction","Likelihood of developing a disease; Allows early intervention"]]},{"id":18102,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"Where does TRAPS's name come from?","o":["TNFR1-associated periodic syndrome","TNF receptor antagonist protein syndrome","T-cell recombinant antibody syndrome","Tumour reactive autoimmune protein syndrome","Transferrin receptor syndrome"],"e":"TRAPS = <b>TNFR1-associated periodic syndrome</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"TRAPS expansion.","rn":[["","TRAPS","TRAPS","TNFR1-associated periodic syndrome"]]},{"id":18103,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"Name the gene mutated in TNFR1-associated periodic syndrome.","e":"TRAPS is caused by TNFRSF1A mutations.","k":"TNFRSF1A.","a":["tnfrsf1a"],"rn":[["","What do TNFRSF1A mutations lead to?","What do TNFRSF1A mutations lead to?","Unprovoked inflammation; TRAPS"],["","Mutations in which gene cause unprovoked inflammation?","Mutations in which gene cause unprovoked inflammation?","TNFRSF1A"]]},{"id":18104,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"Which statement about IL-1-producing cells is correct?","o":["Macrophages, endothelium and epithelium","Macrophages only, not other cell types","Neurons and glia","Hepatocytes only","Fibroblasts only"],"e":"IL-1 is primarily from <b>macrophages</b> but also <b>endothelial and epithelial</b> cells.","w":["Others too.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"IL-1 sources."},{"id":18106,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"Which statement about where stratified medicine sits relative to personalised medicine is correct?","o":["Subgroups must be defined before an individual is placed in one","An individual is analysed in full before any subgroups are defined at all","Stratified and personalised medicine are unconnected","Stratification uses a single patient","Personalised medicine uses no subgroups"],"e":"Student question: does stratified come first? <b>Yes</b>: define subgroups; then analyse any patient, place them in a subgroup and tailor treatment.","w":["Order.","Connected.","Groups.","Uses."],"k":"Stratified before personalised."},{"id":18107,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"Which one of these is <b>not</b> one of the four personalised medicine applications listed?","o":["Surgical robotics","Disease prediction","Pharmacogenetics/pharmacogenomics","Cancer treatment (tumour genotyping)","Infectious agents (pathogen genotyping)"],"e":"Four applications: disease prediction, pharmacogenetics/genomics, cancer treatment, infectious agents.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed."],"k":"Four applications."},{"id":18108,"t":"Translational Research","s":"In clinical research, what are phases 1 and 2 mainly testing?","o":["Phase 1 toxicity; phase 2 efficacy, in small numbers","Phase 1 efficacy; phase 2 toxicity, in small numbers","Phase 1 toxicity; phase 2 efficacy, in thousands","Both phases inform NICE policy directly","Both phases are observational after approval"],"e":"After basic and preclinical research, phase 1 and 2 trials use small numbers of people: phase 1 checks toxicity, phase 2 efficacy. Phase 3 involves more patients and informs policy and practice (e.g. NICE); phase 4 is more observational, reporting back after use.","w":["Swapped.","Small numbers.","Phase 3.","Phase 4."],"k":"Kim et al. 2017.","rn":[["","What is phase 1 of clinical trials?","What is phase 1 of clinical trials?","Small number of patients; First check toxicity"]]},{"id":18109,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"What is a biomarker “baseline”, as used?","o":["Its normal values in health, which disease shifts","Its highest value in disease, used to set the treatment dose","Its value at diagnosis, after which it no longer changes","Its value in a commercial predictive test","Its value in animal models"],"e":"A biomarker has a baseline: its normal values in health. These levels change with pathology or with treatment, which is why biomarkers can be used for diagnosis, prognosis and treatment response, often before anything is clinically visible.","w":["Normal values.","It changes with treatment.","NHS biomarkers differ.","Human data."],"k":"Baliou 2018.","rn":[["","What sets the \"normal\" value of a biomarker?","What sets the \"normal\" value of a biomarker?","A baseline defined in health"]]},{"id":18111,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"A clinician measures a protein whose normal baseline rises in a disease and falls with effective treatment. Which uses of biomarkers are being employed?","o":["Diagnosis and treatment response","Prognosis and gene therapy","Stratification only","Gene editing and CRISPR","ADC targeting"],"e":"Measuring change from baseline in disease (<b>diagnosis</b>) and with treatment (<b>treatment response</b>).","w":["Not.","More.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Biomarker baseline changes.","rn":[["","Biomarkers: 2 uses about treatment?","Biomarkers: 2 uses about treatment?","Predict drug response; Treatment response"],["","What can minute genetic changes influence?","What can minute genetic changes influence?","Response to a treatment"]]},{"id":18112,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"Which explains why molecular subtypes of the same cancer may metastasise differently?","o":["Different molecular causes shape spread","Metastasis is random in all subtypes","All subtypes metastasise identically","Only tumour size determines spread","Subtypes never metastasise"],"e":"The <b>starting cause differs</b>, which also influences how they metastasise, so they will not all respond to the same treatment.","w":["Not.","Different.","Not.","They do."],"k":"Molecular cause shapes metastasis."},{"id":18113,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","s":"Which statement about the CRISPR complex's specificity is correct?","o":["Cas9 with guide RNA binds very specific DNA","Cas9 with guide RNA binds DNA randomly","Cas9 alone binds very specific RNA","Cas9 with an antibody binds proteins","Cas9 binds only RNA"],"e":"Cas9 + guide RNA → a complex that binds <b>very specific</b> sections of DNA.","w":["Specific.","Guide needed.","Not.","DNA."],"k":"CRISPR specificity.","rn":[["","What are the 2 components of CRISPR/Cas9?","What are the 2 components of CRISPR/Cas9?","Cas9 (DNA binding protein); RNA molecule \"the guide\""],["","What happens when Cas9 and the guide bind together?","What happens when Cas9 and the guide bind together?","Form a complex; Binds very specific DNA sections"],["","Image card: What is this complex, and what is it bound to?","What is this complex, and what is it bound to?","CRISPR/Cas9 complex; Cas9 + guide RNA; Bound to DNA"]]},{"id":18114,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","s":"Which statement about the guide RNA's role is correct?","o":["It guides Cas9 to the mutated DNA","It guides the payload to the tumour","It binds and neutralises TNF","It carries the new gene into the nucleus","It is measured as a biomarker"],"e":"A well-designed guide RNA guides the protein to the mutated DNA; the system cuts out the mutation.","w":["ADC.","Not.","Vector.","Not."],"k":"Guide RNA locates mutation.","rn":[["","What does the guide RNA do?","What does the guide RNA do?","Guides Cas9; To mutated DNA"]]},{"id":18115,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","s":"Which statement is correct about gene therapy DNA delivered by adenovirus?","o":["It enters the nucleus without integrating","It integrates permanently into every chromosome","It stays outside the cell surface","It is destroyed before reaching the nucleus","It becomes protein without mRNA"],"e":"Adenovirus introduces DNA into the nucleus, but it is <b>not integrated</b> into a chromosome; the new gene is transcribed into mRNA.","w":["Not integrated.","Not.","Not.","Transcribed."],"k":"Adenoviral DNA not integrated."},{"id":18116,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"Which explains MCB's role in the PR/ER/HER2 story?","o":["Knowing these proteins change in disease made them markers and targets","Knowing these proteins come from viruses made them targets for preventive vaccines","They were found by imaging alone","They are skin proteins without relevance","They are irrelevant to breast cancer"],"e":"PR, ER, BRCA1 and HER2 are proteins present in health whose values change in disease – MCB enables prognostic use and targeted treatment (tamoxifen, Herceptin).","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Relevant."],"k":"MCB → biomarkers and targets.","rn":[["","Are breast cancer biomarkers present in healthy people?","Are breast cancer biomarkers present in healthy people?","Yes: normal proteins; Elevated in disease"],["","Recombinant protein","Recombinant protein","Same as body's protein; Made artificially in lab"],["","Add proteins that change DNA base pairs to Cas9: result?","Add proteins that change DNA base pairs to Cas9: result?","Precise gene editing system"]]},{"id":18117,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"Which statement about biomarkers being proteins is correct?","o":["Mostly our own proteins, changing in disease","Mostly foreign proteins that are absent in health","Never proteins, only DNA","Only lipids in the blood","Fixed values that never change"],"e":"Biomarkers are mostly <b>proteins</b> we all have, with a specific value in health that changes in disease.","w":["Present.","Mostly proteins.","Not.","They change."],"k":"Biomarkers mostly proteins.","rn":[["","What happens to a biomarker value in disease?","What happens to a biomarker value in disease?","Changes from normal"]]},{"id":18118,"t":"Translational Research","s":"Which statement about translational research is correct?","o":["TR: lab → trials → patients → practice → back","Personalised medicine comes before stratification","Anti-TNF is the main treatment for gout","ADCs work by editing genes","Biomarkers are subjective measures"],"e":"Summary: MCB definition; biomarkers objective; TR loop; anti-TNF (RA, AS, psoriasis, PsA, Crohn's, UC); IL-1 blockade; ADCs; stratified then personalised; gene therapy and CRISPR.","w":["After.","IL-1.","Payload.","Objective."],"k":"Summary check.","rn":[["","Translational cycle: first 2 stages?","Translational cycle: first 2 stages?","Lab observations; Clinical trials"],["","What 3 things are in practice-based research?","What 3 things are in practice-based research?","Phase 3 and 4 trials; Observational studies; Survey research"]]},{"id":18119,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"A patient's tumour sequencing reveals a specific mutation that a targeted drug acts on. What illustrates this?","o":["Personalised medicine via tumour genotyping","Stratified medicine via subgroup definition","Gene therapy via adenovirus","A phase 4 trial","IL-1 blockade"],"e":"Tumour sequencing → tailored drug = <b>personalised medicine</b> (cancer treatment application).","w":["Individual.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Tumour genotyping → targeted drug.","rn":[["","Personalised medicine: cancer treatment","Personalised medicine: cancer treatment","Tumour genotyping; Precise characterisation and treatment options"],["","Personalised cancer medicine: first 3 steps?","Personalised cancer medicine: first 3 steps?","Cancer patients; Tumour; Sequencing"]]},{"id":18120,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"Which list contains only conditions treated with anti-TNF therapy?","o":["RA, ankylosing spondylitis, psoriasis, Crohn’s, UC","RA, ankylosing spondylitis, gout, Crohn’s, UC","Still’s disease, Schnitzler syndrome, gout","Hidradenitis suppurativa, type 2 diabetes, psoriasis","RA, Still’s disease, Crohn’s, hidradenitis suppurativa"],"e":"Anti-TNF is used for rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, psoriasis, psoriatic arthritis, Crohn’s disease and ulcerative colitis. IL-1 blockade is used for Still’s disease, Schnitzler syndrome, hidradenitis suppurativa, gout and type 2 diabetes.","w":["Gout is an IL-1 disease.","IL-1 diseases.","Mostly IL-1.","Still’s and HS are IL-1."],"k":"Lecturer contact.","rn":[["","Anti-TNF: which 3 other diseases?","Anti-TNF: which 3 other diseases?","Psoriasis; Crohn's disease; Ulcerative colitis"],["","Which 3 joint diseases are treated with anti-TNF?","Which 3 joint diseases are treated with anti-TNF?","Rheumatoid arthritis; Ankylosing spondylitis; Psoriatic arthritis"],["","Ankylosing spondylitis can be treated with which biological?","Ankylosing spondylitis can be treated with which biological?","Anti-TNF"]]},{"id":18121,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"Two women of the same age have breast cancer of similar size. Tumour A is ER-positive and PR-positive and HER2-negative. Tumour B is ER-negative, PR-negative and HER2-positive. Both are untreated at the time of testing.<br><br>Which patient is likely to have the better prognosis, and why?","o":["A: ER/PR positivity means better survival; HER2 positivity in B means a more aggressive cancer","B: HER2 positivity means better survival than ER/PR positivity","A: but only because HER2-negative tumours are always benign","B: ER-negative and PR-negative tumours have the highest survival rate of all breast cancer types","Both equal: biomarkers do not predict prognosis"],"e":"Step 1: PR-positive and ER-positive patients have better survival than PR-negative and ER-negative patients. Step 2: HER2-positive cancers have a worse prognosis and are more aggressive than HER2-negative cancers, so patient A is likely to do better.","w":["HER2 positivity means a worse, not better, prognosis.","HER2-negative tumours are still cancers.","ER/PR negativity is linked to worse survival.","Prognosis is one of the clinical uses of biomarkers."],"k":"ER+/PR+ → better survival; HER2+ → worse, more aggressive.","rn":[["","Which 2 breast cancer biomarkers mean better survival when positive?","Which 2 breast cancer biomarkers mean better survival when positive?","PR; ER"],["","HER2-positive vs HER2-negative patients","HER2-positive vs HER2-negative patients","HER2-positive: worse prognosis; More aggressive cancer"]]},{"id":18122,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"An oncology service first divides all its breast cancer patients into PR/ER-positive, HER2-positive and triple-negative groups. Later, for one particular patient, it sequences her tumour's genes and chooses a drug for her alone based on the result.<br><br>Which approaches are used in the two steps, and in which order?","o":["Stratified medicine first, then personalised medicine","Personalised medicine first, then stratified medicine","Both steps are translational research only","Both steps are stratified medicine","Personalised medicine first, as it starts from a large group with the same disease, then gene therapy"],"e":"Step 1: stratified medicine identifies subgroups of a population with distinct disease characteristics or treatment responses, as with the breast cancer subgroups. Step 2: personalised medicine is a tailor-made model where therapy is delivered on an individualised basis, as in sequencing one patient's tumour to choose her drug; it builds on stratification.","w":["Stratification of the group comes first.","The steps describe applying subgroups and individual therapy, not research.","Choosing a drug for one patient is personalised.","Stratified medicine starts from the large group."],"k":"Stratified: subgroups; personalised: individual therapy, after stratification.","rn":[["","Which comes first: stratified or personalised medicine?","Which comes first: stratified or personalised medicine?","Stratified (define subgroups first)"],["","Stratified medicine","Stratified medicine","Identifies population subgroups; Distinct disease or response"],["","Personalised cancer medicine: first 3 steps?","Personalised cancer medicine: first 3 steps?","Cancer patients; Tumour; Sequencing"]]},{"id":18123,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"A 35-year-old man has Crohn's disease that has not responded to standard treatment. His gastroenterologist starts a biological therapy that neutralises a cytokine made mainly by macrophages, which normally binds TNFR1 and TNFR2 to start inflammation.<br><br>Which therapy is this, and what other conditions is it used for?","o":["Anti-TNF; rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, psoriasis, psoriatic arthritis, UC","IL-1 blockade; gout, Still's disease and type 2 diabetes","An antibody–drug conjugate; HER2-positive breast cancer","CRISPR/Cas9; editing the TNF gene","Anti-IL1R1; hidradenitis suppurativa and Schnitzler syndrome, because IL-1 also binds TNFR1"],"e":"Step 1: tumour necrosis factor (TNF) is a cytokine mainly generated by macrophages that binds TNFR1 and TNFR2 to initiate inflammation, and an antibody against it neutralises it. Step 2: anti-TNF biological therapies are used for rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, psoriasis, psoriatic arthritis, Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis.","w":["IL-1 binds IL-1RI, not TNF receptors.","ADCs deliver drugs to cancer cells.","CRISPR is gene editing, not a cytokine-neutralising therapy.","IL-1 binds IL-1RI, not TNFR1."],"k":"Anti-TNF: RA, AS, psoriasis, PsA, Crohn's, UC.","rn":[["","Which 3 joint diseases are treated with anti-TNF?","Which 3 joint diseases are treated with anti-TNF?","Rheumatoid arthritis; Ankylosing spondylitis; Psoriatic arthritis"],["","Anti-TNF: which 3 joint/skin diseases?","Anti-TNF: which 3 joint/skin diseases?","Rheumatoid arthritis; Ankylosing spondylitis; Psoriatic arthritis"]]},{"id":18124,"t":"TNF and IL-1","s":"A 50-year-old man with gout has repeated flares driven by a cytokine made by macrophages, endothelial and epithelial cells, which binds IL-1RI. His rheumatologist discusses two drugs. Drug X is an antibody that binds the receptor itself. Drug Y is a recombinant \"decoy\" that binds the cytokine so it cannot reach the receptor.<br><br>Which statement correctly describes drugs X and Y?","o":["X is anti-IL1R1, blocking the receptor; Y is a recombinant IL-1 receptor antagonist mopping up IL-1","X neutralises TNF; Y blocks TNFR2","X is an antibody–drug conjugate; Y is CRISPR/Cas9","X mops up IL-1; Y blocks the IL-1 receptor directly","Both are anti-TNF drugs, because IL-1 and TNF bind the same receptors on macrophages"],"e":"Step 1: interleukin-1 (IL-1) is a cytokine made mainly by macrophages, but also endothelial and epithelial cells, that binds IL-1 receptor type 1 (IL-1RI) to start inflammation, and gout is one of the IL-1 diseases listed. Step 2: IL-1 blockade can use anti-IL1R1 (an antibody binding the receptor) or a recombinant IL-1 receptor antagonist (a decoy that mops up the protein).","w":["These drugs act on IL-1, not TNF.","ADCs and CRISPR are not IL-1 blockers.","The descriptions are swapped.","IL-1 and TNF bind different receptors."],"k":"IL-1 blockade: anti-IL1R1 (receptor) or recombinant IL-1Ra (decoy).","rn":[["","What are the 2 IL-1 blockade treatment options?","What are the 2 IL-1 blockade treatment options?","Anti-IL1R1; Recombinant IL-1 receptor antagonist"],["","How does anti-IL1R1 work?","How does anti-IL1R1 work?","Antibody blocks the IL-1 receptor; IL-1 cannot bind"]]},{"id":18125,"t":"Biomarkers","s":"A patient has a blood protein measured at diagnosis of a disease to confirm it. The same protein is measured again after starting a drug, and the level falls. Over the next year, rising levels warn of worsening disease. The doctor also uses a baseline result to estimate the likely outcome.<br><br>Which statement best describes this protein?","o":["A biomarker used for diagnosis, treatment response, progression and prognosis","A translational research trial in phase 4","A stratified subgroup of the population","A CRISPR guide RNA targeting PAM sequences in the patient's genome","A gene therapy vector"],"e":"Step 1: a biomarker is a characteristic that is objectively measured and evaluated as an indicator of normal biological processes, pathogenic processes or pharmacologic responses to a therapeutic intervention. Step 2: clinical uses of biomarkers include diagnosis, prognosis, prediction of drug response, disease progression and treatment response, all of which are shown here.","w":["Phase 4 is observational study of a treatment in use, not a measured protein.","A subgroup is a set of patients, not a measured protein.","A guide RNA is part of a gene-editing system.","A vector delivers a gene; it is not measured as an indicator."],"k":"Biomarkers: diagnosis, prognosis, drug response, progression, treatment response.","rn":[["","Biomarkers: 3 uses about disease?","Biomarkers: 3 uses about disease?","Diagnosis; Prognosis; Disease progression"]]},{"id":18126,"t":"Translational Research","s":"A new drug discovered in the lab is first given to a small number of patients to check for toxicity and then efficacy. It is later tested in many more patients, reported to health services and approved, changing practice. After it comes into routine use, doctors continue to collect observational data about it.<br><br>Which phases are described, in order, and what is this overall process called?","o":["Phases 1–2, phase 3, phase 4; translational research","Phase 4, phase 3, phase 1; stratified medicine","Phase 3, phases 1–2, phase 4; personalised medicine","Phases 1–2, phase 4, phase 3; gene therapy","Phase 1, phase 2, phase 3; molecular cell biology, which ends once a drug is approved"],"e":"Step 1: translational research is applying basic research to a clinical setting. Step 2: phases 1 (and 2) use a small number of patients to test toxicity and then efficacy; phase 3 uses more patients and may change policy and practice (approvals); phase 4 is observational study after the treatment is in use, giving continuous feedback.","w":["The order is reversed and the process is not stratification.","Phases 1–2 come before phase 3.","Phase 3 comes before phase 4.","Observational follow-up after approval is phase 4."],"k":"TR: lab → phases 1–2 → phase 3 → phase 4 observation.","rn":[["","TR","TR","Translational research"],["","What 3 things are in practice-based research?","What 3 things are in practice-based research?","Phase 3 and 4 trials; Observational studies; Survey research"],["","What is in bedside (human clinical) research?","What is in bedside (human clinical) research?","Controlled observational studies; Phase 3 trials; Health services studies"]]},{"id":18127,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","s":"A research team wants to correct a single faulty base pair in a gene in a patient's cells. They design an RNA molecule matching the target DNA next to a 5′-NGG-3′ sequence and combine it with a DNA-binding protein. They add another protein that can change DNA base pairs.<br><br>Which system are they using, and what are its two core components?","o":["CRISPR/Cas9: the Cas9 protein and a guide RNA","Gene therapy: a retroviral vector and a functional gene","An antibody–drug conjugate: an antibody and a cytotoxic drug","IL-1 blockade: a receptor antibody and a decoy","Stratified medicine: a biomarker and a patient subgroup, chosen by sequencing the tumour"],"e":"Step 1: CRISPR/Cas9 has two components: a DNA-binding protein called Cas9 and an RNA molecule known as the guide; together they bind very specific sections of DNA, with the guide targeting PAM sequences 5′-NGG-3′. Step 2: adding proteins that selectively change DNA base pairs makes it a precise gene-editing system, while adding proteins that turn genes on and off makes a gene-control system.","w":["Gene therapy replaces a defective gene rather than editing a base pair.","ADCs deliver drugs to cancer cells.","IL-1 blockade is a cytokine therapy.","Stratified medicine defines subgroups and does not edit genes."],"k":"CRISPR = Cas9 + guide RNA (PAM 5′-NGG-3′) → editing or gene control.","rn":[["","What are the 2 components of CRISPR/Cas9?","What are the 2 components of CRISPR/Cas9?","Cas9 (DNA binding protein); RNA molecule \"the guide\""]]},{"id":18128,"t":"Gene Therapy and CRISPR","s":"A child has a disease caused by a non-working copy of a gene. One approach delivers a working copy of the whole gene into the child's cells using a viral vector. A second approach instead targets the faulty gene directly and corrects the specific error in place.<br><br>Which approaches are these?","o":["First: gene therapy (replacement); second: gene editing with CRISPR/Cas9","First: CRISPR/Cas9; second: gene therapy","Both: antibody–drug conjugates","First: stratified medicine; second: personalised medicine","First: gene therapy; second: anti-TNF therapy, which neutralises the faulty protein"],"e":"Step 1: gene therapy replaces a defective gene with a functional one, and enhancements include the use of retroviral elements (viral vectors). Step 2: alternatives include CRISPR, a precise gene-editing system that binds very specific DNA sections and can selectively change base pairs.","w":["The two approaches are swapped.","ADCs target drugs to cancer cells, not genes.","These are ways of grouping and treating patients, not genetic therapies.","Anti-TNF neutralises a cytokine, not a faulty gene."],"k":"Gene therapy: replace gene; CRISPR: edit gene in place.","rn":[["","What is gene addition in CRISPR/Cas9 editing?","What is gene addition in CRISPR/Cas9 editing?","Adding new DNA"],["","Gene therapy","Gene therapy","Replace defective gene; With a functional one"],["","Add proteins that change DNA base pairs to Cas9: result?","Add proteins that change DNA base pairs to Cas9: result?","Precise gene editing system"]]},{"id":18129,"t":"Antibody-Drug Conjugates","s":"A 55-year-old woman has a cancer whose cells carry a particular surface protein in large amounts. Her oncologist offers a drug in which an antibody against that protein is linked to a toxic payload. The aim is to deliver the toxin mainly to the cancer cells rather than to healthy tissue.<br><br>What type of therapy is this, and what is its main advantage?","o":["An antibody–drug conjugate; improved targeting and therapeutic efficacy","Gene therapy; replacing the cancer's defective gene","A recombinant receptor antagonist; mopping up a cytokine","Anti-TNF therapy; reducing inflammation in the tumour","CRISPR/Cas9; switching off all genes in the patient's body except the cancer genes"],"e":"Step 1: antibody–drug conjugates (ADCs) are a translational research example of selective targeting of cancer cells. Step 2: ADCs are a unique approach that attempts to improve drug delivery targeting and therefore overall therapeutic efficacy, delivering a cytotoxic payload to cells carrying the target.","w":["The drug delivers a toxin, not a gene.","A receptor antagonist blocks a cytokine, not a cancer cell.","Anti-TNF neutralises a cytokine and is not a toxin carrier.","CRISPR edits specific DNA; it does not switch off all genes."],"k":"ADCs: antibody + payload → better targeting and efficacy.","rn":[["","What is the aim of antibody-drug conjugates?","What is the aim of antibody-drug conjugates?","Improve drug delivery targeting; So improve therapeutic efficacy"],["","Selective targeting of cancer cells is an example of what?","Selective targeting of cancer cells is an example of what?","Translational research; Antibody-drug conjugate therapy"]]},{"id":18130,"t":"Stratified and Personalised Medicine","s":"Four patients have genomic tests. Patient 1 learns their likelihood of developing a disease so they can act early. Patient 2 has a drug dose adjusted based on their genes. Patient 3 has their tumour genotyped to choose treatment. Patient 4 has the infecting organism genotyped to choose a targeted therapy.<br><br>Which applications of personalised medicine are shown?","o":["Disease prediction, pharmacogenetics, cancer treatment and pathogen genotyping","Stratified subgroups, gene therapy, CRISPR and ADCs","Diagnosis, prognosis, progression and treatment response only","Phases 1, 2, 3 and 4 of a clinical trial","Disease prediction, gene therapy, anti-TNF therapy and IL-1 blockade, in that order"],"e":"Step 1: genomic data applications of personalised medicine include disease prediction (likelihood of developing a disease, allowing early intervention) and pharmacogenetics/pharmacogenomics (altering dose and treatment). Step 2: they also include cancer treatment (tumour genotyping for precise characterisation and treatment options) and infectious agents (pathogen genotyping for targeted therapies and vaccines).","w":["These are other therapies and approaches, not genomic applications.","These are biomarker uses, not the four genomic applications.","These are trial phases.","Only disease prediction fits; the others are therapies."],"k":"Personalised genomics: prediction, pharmacogenetics, tumour and pathogen genotyping.","rn":[["","Genomic personalised medicine: 2 uses?","Genomic personalised medicine: 2 uses?","Disease prediction; Pharmacogenetics/genomics"],["","Personalised medicine: cancer treatment","Personalised medicine: cancer treatment","Tumour genotyping; Precise characterisation and treatment options"],["","Personalised medicine: infectious agents","Personalised medicine: infectious agents","Pathogen genotyping; Precise targeted therapies; Potential vaccines"]]}]);
